                        "email": "eldin.lee@quantatw.com",
                        "username": "eldin-lee"
                    },
                    "message": "Thanks for the suggestion. I\u2019ll replace them with the cable inventory entries from entity-manager:\n/xyz/openbmc_project/inventory/system/cable/ReturnValve\n/xyz/openbmc_project/inventory/system/cable/SupplyValve"
                },
                {
                    "file": "meta-facebook/meta-ventura/recipes-phosphor/sensors/dbus-sensors/critical-leak-handler",
                    "line": 27,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Eldin Lee",
                        "email": "eldin.lee@quantatw.com",
                        "username": "eldin-lee"
                    },
                    "message": "It has already been implemented."
                },
                {
                    "file": "meta-facebook/meta-ventura/recipes-phosphor/sensors/dbus-sensors/critical-leak-handler",
                    "line": 29,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Jagpal S Gill",
                        "email": "paligill@gmail.com",
                        "username": "jagpalgill"
                    },
                    "message": "This is Valve open/close status and it shouldn't be modeled as inventory item. If that is what you need to check, then better to use the old method for now until the valve-monitor is merged upstream which will expose the valve open/close status as a sensor."
                },
                {
                    "file": "meta-facebook/meta-ventura/recipes-phosphor/sensors/dbus-sensors/critical-leak-handler",
                    "line": 29,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Jason Hsu",
                        "email": "jason-hsu@quantatw.com",
                        "username": "QuantaJasonHsu"
                    },
                    "message": "Which old method are you referring to? Thanks."
                },
                {
                    "file": "meta-facebook/meta-ventura/recipes-phosphor/sensors/dbus-sensors/critical-leak-handler",
                    "line": 29,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Jason Hsu",
                        "email": "jason-hsu@quantatw.com",
                        "username": "QuantaJasonHsu"
                    },
                    "message": "\u4f60\u6307\u7684"
                },
                {
                    "file": "meta-facebook/meta-ventura/recipes-phosphor/sensors/dbus-sensors/critical-leak-handler",
                    "line": 29,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Jagpal S Gill",
                        "email": "paligill@gmail.com",
                        "username": "jagpalgill"
                    },
                    "message": "Why can't you directly read the open/close status GPIO? Why do you need to model a VT open/close status as inventory item (as its not inventory item but a status).Does anything else also needs this info to be read from DBus?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "meta-facebook/meta-ventura/recipes-phosphor/sensors/dbus-sensors/critical-leak-handler",
                    "line": 29,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Eldin Lee",
                        "email": "eldin.lee@quantatw.com",
                        "username": "eldin-lee"
                    },
                    "message": "@paligill@gmail.com In the first commit, @jason-hsu@quantatw.com used devmem to read the GPIO so it wouldn\u2019t be blocked. Later, he followed the suggestion to avoid hard-coded addresses, so he tried using the available D-Bus service instead.\n\nI added [gpio_util](https://gerrit.openbmc.org/c/openbmc/openbmc/+/82712/6/meta-facebook/meta-ventura/recipes-ventura/plat-svc/files/gpio_util)(see Gerrit [#82712](https://gerrit.openbmc.org/c/openbmc/openbmc/+/82712)) which uses gpioget, and if the line is busy, it falls back to debugfs.\n\nI also switched the implementation to use get_gpio_val_by_name:\n```\n$(get_gpio_val_by_name \"RETURN_CNTL_FB_D_R\")\n$(get_gpio_val_by_name \"SUPPLY_CNTL_FB_D_R\")\n$(get_gpio_val_by_name \"RPU_READY_PLD_R\")\n```\n\nCould you take a look when you have time?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "meta-facebook/meta-ventura/recipes-phosphor/sensors/dbus-sensors/critical-leak-handler",
                    "line": 29,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Jagpal S Gill",
                        "email": "paligill@gmail.com",
                        "username": "jagpalgill"
                    },
                    "message": "Is anything else monitoring these gpios in the system which will cause the gpioget to fail?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "meta-facebook/meta-ventura/recipes-phosphor/sensors/dbus-sensors/critical-leak-handler",
                    "line": 29,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Eldin Lee",
                        "email": "eldin.lee@quantatw.com",
                        "username": "eldin-lee"
                    },
                    "message": "As far as I know, we need to log the VT ON/OFF events (RETURN_CNTL_FB_D_R and SUPPLY_CNTL_FB_D_R), but I am not sure which method will be used to log these VT states.\n\nSince RPU_READY_PLD_R is already used by gpio-monitor, running gpioget on this line will fail with a busy error. That\u2019s why I implemented [gpio_util](https://gerrit.openbmc.org/c/openbmc/openbmc/+/82712/6/meta-facebook/meta-ventura/recipes-ventura/plat-svc/files/gpio_util) (see Gerrit [#82712](https://gerrit.openbmc.org/c/openbmc/openbmc/+/82712)) for this purpose.\n\nFYI.\n```bash\nroot@bmc:~# gpioinfo | grep RPU_READY_PLD_R\n        line  30: \"RPU_READY_PLD_R\" \"gpio_monitor\" input active-high [used]\nroot@bmc:~# gpioget 2 30; echo $?\ngpioget: error reading GPIO values: Device or resource busy\n1\n```\nIf you prefer not to use gpio_util, we could revert to using devmon to read directly from the register. If you have a better approach in mind, I\u2019d appreciate it if you could share it with us."
                },
                {
                    "file": "meta-facebook/meta-ventura/recipes-phosphor/sensors/dbus-sensors/critical-leak-handler",
                    "line": 29,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Jagpal S Gill",
                        "email": "paligill@gmail.com",
                        "username": "jagpalgill"
                    },
                    "message": "@eldin.lee@quantatw.com You can use the gpio_util method you have defined for now (until we move to valve-monitor solution). I have provided some comments on the other PR. Feel free to change the order of PRs and bring gpio_util in this patch since its ready to go."
                },
                {
                    "file": "meta-facebook/meta-ventura/recipes-phosphor/sensors/dbus-sensors/critical-leak-handler",
                    "line": 29,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Eldin Lee",
                        "email": "eldin.lee@quantatw.com",
                        "username": "eldin-lee"
                    },
                    "message": "We appreciate the time and effort you spent reviewing this PR. I\u2019ve included [gpio_util](https://gerrit.openbmc.org/c/openbmc/openbmc/+/81627/6/meta-facebook/meta-ventura/recipes-ventura/plat-svc/files/gpio_util) in this PR. When the valve-monitor solution is completed, please inform us so we can align."
                }
            ],
